  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Indio, Texas was founded by the Cazneau family, who secured land from Mexican grants in 1833 and built the ranch that became the town’s heart.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/indio-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By 1910 the ranch boasted a drilled well and basic infrastructure, allowing it to support a small but thriving community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/indio-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1912, turning the ranch into a local mail hub for the surrounding area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/indio-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Environmental hardships and economic decline pushed the town into abandonment by 1936, though preservation efforts began in the 1960s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/indio-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Even after most residents left, Indio remained a voting precinct for up to forty people well into the 1950s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/indio-tx-zavala-county"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Indio sits eight miles east of Crystal City in south‑central Zavala County, near Tortuga Creek and the Nueces River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
